In this lesson, we'll look at why trig ratios stay the same for any given angle, no matter how big or small the triangle is. Here's the key idea. A trig ratio is just a comparison of two sides of a right triangle, so it depends on the shape of the triangle, not its size. Picture a right triangle with one acute angle, and then imagine stretching or shrinking that whole triangle by some scale factor. Every side gets multiplied by that same factor, so the opposite side is longer, the adjacent side is longer, and the hypotenuse is longer, all by exactly the same amount. Now here's the crucial step. When you form a ratio of any two of those sides, that common factor cancels out, and what you're left with is the same number every time. That's why sine, cosine, and tangent only depend on the angle itself, not on how big your triangle happens to be. And that's exactly why a trig table works for every right triangle in the world. Now let's see it in action.